// The emitter must FAIL CLOSED when a generic callee has no resolved instantiation.
//
// It used to fail open. `emitInvocation` fell past the instantiation block to the ordinary function path,
// which emits the TEMPLATE's own mangled C name — a name no body ever defines, because only instances are
// emitted. So kama reported success and clang refused the generated C. Measured on 0.9.99, on this exact
// program: `error: call to undeclared function '_Fm2__ident'` on the line `__self.v = _Fm2__ident(7);`.
// That is the failure mode that hid an entire family of discovery holes, because it surfaced on a line of
// C rather than on the line the author wrote.
//
// The shape below is the one live trigger left: a field DEFAULT INITIALIZER is not walked by either
// discovery pass (`collectGenericInsts` and `collectCollections` both read a field's declared TYPE only,
// never its initializer expression), so the call is never discovered. That is a tracked limitation — see
// docs/ROADMAP.md's "Remaining language limitations" row — and the point of this fixture is not the
// limitation but the DIAGNOSTIC: while it stands, it must be a kama error naming the callee, never
// invalid C. Write the turbofish, or call from a body, and it compiles.
fn T ident<T>(T x) { return x; }
type value B {
    public int32 v = ident(x: 7);   // ERROR: no instantiation resolved for this call
    public ctor make() { }
}
fn int32 main() { return B.make().v - 7; }
